WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.034 --> 00:00:03.920
- This might be the most
ambitious long shot precision

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.160
experiment that I've ever seen.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.040
Scientists have put one
atomic clock down in Boulder,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.240
Colorado, and one up in this
observatory 4,000 meters

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.600
above sea level, and they're going to try

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.760
and measure the difference
in time dilation

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.000
according to general relativity.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.120
Because up here, time itself
should be passing faster

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.160
relative to down in Boulder.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.440
- If you go into a science center,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.760
often they will explain
gravity as the bending

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.000
of space time, and what they
will have is a little funnel

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.640
where the particle goes around the funnel,

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.880
and that's meant to give you an impression

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.200
of why the earth goes around the sun.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.120
But that's not actually what happens.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.840
It's not as much that
space is curving, it's

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.200
that time is stretching.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.480
So the dominant effect of gravity that we

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.920
experience is the slowing
down of time as we get closer

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.680
to a massive object.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.440
- This is one of the predictions

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.080
of Einstein's general
theory of relativity,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.440
and it actually predicts

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.200
how much faster the mountain
clock should be ticking.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.720
Put one clock in Boulder and
another 2.7 kilometers higher,

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.680
and the mountain clock should
gain about 25 billionths

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.000
of a second every day.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.680
This effect of general relativity

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.640
and time dilation has
been proven in experiments

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.000
time and time again.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.760
So why are we still testing it?

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.560
Because we know that at some
quantum scale, this theory

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.680
of gravity is going to stop working.

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:25.200
- The way we know there
is some contradiction is

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.880
that we know that particles
can be in the superposition

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:31.360
of two places here and there.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.120
That's what quantum mechanics tells us.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.160
And what Einstein's theory of
general relativity tells us is

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:38.000
that if a particle is here,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.400
it bends space time in a certain way.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.440
And if it's there, it bends
space time in a certain way.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.400
- So what happens to
spacetime if a particle

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.040
with mass is in two places at once?

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.360
- We don't have an
answer to that question.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.840
Einstein's theory of general
relativity is silent on the

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:54.800
question of what happens

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.880
to spacetime when a particle
is in superposition.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.960
- And that leaves us with one

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.680
of the biggest unanswered
questions in physics.

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.760
Does gravity itself obey the
rules of quantum mechanics?

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:08.800
So teams around the world are
trying to pin down exactly

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.800
where gravity might stop
following Einstein's prediction.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.880
And they want to find out
if there's a correction term

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.320
needed in the equations

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.160
that might completely change our

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.360
understanding of the universe.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.320
Which means three massive challenges.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.560
One, build a clock so accurate
it can track time to 18

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.080
decimal places.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.160
Two, take that clock out of
the lab and into the sky.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.520
And three, find a way
to read a signal so fast

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.000
that electronics can't even process it.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.160
This is the National
Institute of Standards

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:47.280
and Technology in Boulder, Colorado.

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.840
NIST has an atomic clock
that is so accurate

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.400
that if it started counting
when the universe began,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.920
it would not have lost or
gained a second up until now.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.080
NIST has been home to
pretty much every kind

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.280
of atomic clock ever made
from tiny chip scale clocks

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.120
and the most accurate clock on earth

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:07.200
to the first experimental
clock from way back in 1949.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.560
And it's that accuracy

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:10.640
that makes atomic clocks powerful tools

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.640
for testing fundamental physics.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.960
This little survey marker
is actually in every room

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:16.800
that has an atomic clock at NIST,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.680
and it's the official telling
of which altitude you're at

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.240
above sea level so that you
can take it into account

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.400
for when you're aligning
the atomic clocks.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.840
They even connect to clocks

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.880
that are 400 kilometers off
the earth's surface on the

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.240
International Space Station
through this little dome here.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.040
Now, you might think that
an atomic clock runs on

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.160
radioactivity because

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.960
after all, we use radioactive
decay in the half-life

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:41.600
of elements to date things
that happened billions

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.680
of years ago and we can
do that with an error

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.000
of less than 1%, but that is
not how an atomic clock works.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.680
For keeping time, we need something

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.080
that will oscillate back and forth.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.400
For a grandfather clock, it's a pendulum

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.560
and for a wristwatch,
it's a piece of quartz

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.360
that is vibrating under
an electric current.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:02.800
But an atomic clock gets its
frequency from a fundamental

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.520
property of the atom itself.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:08.960
Electrons can only occupy
specific energy levels

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:11.200
and they can jump from
one level to another

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.600
by absorbing a photon of light.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.360
But this only happens when a
photon's energy matches the

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.680
energy difference between the two levels

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.960
and the energy of a photon
depends on its frequency.

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:25.920
So you can use a laser,
tune its frequency,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.040
and that will change the
energy of its photons.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.520
Now, most frequencies are simply ignored,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:34.480
but when the frequency of
such a laser is just right,

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:38.240
you get this transition and
the electron makes the jump.

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.240
And that tells us the atom's
natural resonant frequency.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.440
Now we can tune the laser to
match that frequency exactly,

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:48.880
and it's the oscillations
of this laser light that act

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.200
as the atomic clock's pendulum.

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.520
- So this is NIST F3.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.000
It's one of the Caesium
fountains we operate here at the

00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:58.080
National Institute of
Standards and Technology.

00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.520
We use these machines in order

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.040
to experimentally realize the
definition of the SI second.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.720
- What is the exact frequency?
- Count

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.800
to 9,192,631,770.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:09.920
And after you've counted that high,

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.400
you've counted exactly one second.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.280
- This clock feeds into a network

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.840
that helps determine coordinated
universal time or UTC.

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.480
It's the standard that keeps
your phone, your computer,

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.960
and much of the world synchronized.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:26.640
Now, since 1967, the time it takes

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.240
for this exact number of
Caesium 133 oscillations

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:34.160
to get past your detector has
been the official definition

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:35.920
of one second.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:37.360
Keeping clocks synchronized

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:40.320
with each other is a centuries
old problem, and that's

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.280
because every oscillator
has inconsistencies in

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:44.720
the way that they're made.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.120
But for atoms, it doesn't matter

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:48.720
where you get the atom from.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:51.840
The natural resonant frequency
is the same for all atoms

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.640
of the same element across the universe.

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:55.680
And it's the arrangement

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.560
of these electrons in particular isotopes

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.680
that make a very good atomic clock.

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:06.400
So these atoms give us ticks
that are incredibly consistent.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.520
How reliably one tick
matches the next is the

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:11.040
clock's stability.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.440
Some elements are more stable than others,

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.200
which is why Caesium 133 is really popular.

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:18.720
But there's another challenge.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:21.360
It's one thing for every
tick to be consistent.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.320
It's another to know that
you're measuring the atom's

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:26.080
true ticking rate.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:28.640
In other words, how
close is our measurement

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.880
to the true atomic frequency?

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:32.640
That is accuracy.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.840
And unlike the atom itself,
accuracy comes down to

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:38.080
how well the clock is built.

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:42.720
Now, the best caesium atomic
clocks are over 50 billion times

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.120
more accurate than the quartz
crystal in your wristwatch.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:50.080
The overall performance of
a clock can be described by

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:51.840
how many seconds it will gain

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:56.720
or lose in a given time expressed
as an order of magnitude.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:00.800
So what order of magnitude
is the caesium clock

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.880
that we're now using to standardize time?

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:06.640
- Those realize fractionally
about a part in 10

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.640
to the 16 of accuracy.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.400
So over the course of
maybe tens of millions

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:14.880
or a hundred million
years, it would only lose

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.160
of order a second.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.600
- But this is still
not good enough to find

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:21.360
where gravity might stray from the

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:23.760
predictions of general relativity.

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:26.480
If you want a more accurate
clock, you can start

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:29.200
with finding a higher resonant frequency.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:32.320
Because the more oscillations
you can pack into a second,

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:33.840
the more finally you can define it.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.880
And it turns out that elements
have different natural

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.360
frequencies, so you just
have to find one that fits.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:42.400
And the perfect candidate
for the job is ytterbium.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.440
- I like to say it
looks a little bit like,

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:47.680
like iron pyrite, like fool's gold.

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:50.640
- While caesium clocks
operate in the microwave part

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:53.360
of the spectrum, ytterbium
resonates in the optical band

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:57.040
with a frequency 56,000 times higher.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:08:02.400
This equates to 518 trillion
oscillations every second,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:05.840
and that allows for
accuracies of one part in 10

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.520
to the power of 18.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:09.280
So this clock won't gain

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:15.440
or lose a second in more
than the age of the universe.

00:08:15.440 --> 00:08:19.040
But when the ytterbium
is solid, the atoms are

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:22.560
so close together that neighboring
atoms pull each other's

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.520
electrons out of place, and this broadens

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:28.640
and blurs the frequency at
which the electrons make the

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:32.400
transition, and that makes
the natural resonant frequency

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:34.960
pretty much impossible to find.

00:08:34.960 --> 00:08:38.000
So ytterbium first has
to be turned into a gas.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.560
- There's an oven where
we heat this up - Yeah.

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.880
To about 500 Celsius.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:44.880
We make a vapor of it,

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.680
and then at the end of the
oven, there's actually a hole

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.600
where just a, a stream of
this stuff comes shooting out.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.240
- But now these atoms are traveling at

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:56.000
around 300 meters per second,

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.680
which causes a different problem.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.600
If an atom is moving towards

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.920
or away from a photon as it's hit,

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.200
it will absorb the light
at a different frequency.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.040
This is called a Doppler shift,

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.160
and it alters the reading of
the natural resonant frequency

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:12.080
by one part in a millionth.

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:16.400
An error at the sixth digit
when you're aiming for 18 digits

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:19.680
of accuracy would end
the experiment there.

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.280
So the team has to find a way

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.440
to slow down the atom's
movement once they've

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.754
reached the main vacuum chamber.

00:09:25.754 --> 00:09:29.120
- You have to cool them down
in three dimensions in free

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:32.640
space, and so that requires
having these laser beams in

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:33.680
three-dimension as well.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:36.240
So the blue laser operates first.

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:39.760
It takes the hot beam of
atoms that are at hundreds

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:41.040
of Kelvin temperature

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:44.640
and brings them all the way
down to about a millikelvin

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.600
or a thousandth of a
degree above absolute zero.

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.280
And then it trades off

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:53.280
with the green laser also
propagating along the same axes,

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.240
and that takes those
millikelvin temperature atoms

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.320
and brings them down to
about a microkelvin or so.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.520
- It might seem counterintuitive
that you can cool atoms

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.200
by firing lasers at them,

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.000
but what the scientists are
doing is using different

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.360
electron transitions inside ytterbium 171.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.640
They're now taking advantage
of that Doppler shift.

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.400
The lasers are tuned just

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:18.400
below the frequency the atoms absorb,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.880
so an atom only absorbs light from a

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.120
beam it's moving towards.

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:24.960
And because light carries momentum,

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.600
each photon it absorbs gives
it a tiny kick slowing it down.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.640
The atom then emits a photon
in a random direction,

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:34.000
so over many cycles,

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:37.200
those random emissions
average out while the absorbed

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:39.760
photons keep slowing the atoms down.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:42.720
Overall, the lasers
can cool the atoms down

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:46.640
to just a millionth of a
degree above absolute zero.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.320
Now, once the atoms are cooled,

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.880
the next challenge is
keeping them in one place,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:53.680
and it turns out you need
a laser for this too.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.560
- We'll focus a beam
into our vacuum system

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.640
where the ytterbium atoms are located,

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:02.480
and then we'll actually
just put a mirror there

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:04.720
to reflect it back onto itself.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:07.280
And if we could kind of
zoom in enough, we'd see

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:09.840
that there's this interference pattern

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:12.560
between the two directions
that leads to nodes

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.040
and anti-nodes of laser intensity.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.480
- The outgoing and reflected
light waves overlap,

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.720
and in some places they
cancel each other out

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.560
and in others they reinforce each other.

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.280
And the result is a
repeating pattern called a

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:27.600
standing wave.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.640
Now, the ytterbium 171 atom is neutral.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:33.440
The charges of its protons
and electrons balance out,

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:36.480
but light is an electromagnetic wave.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:39.120
So the laser beam carries
an electric field,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:43.120
and that electric field pulls
those charges very slightly

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:44.880
in opposite directions.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:46.800
This is called an induced dipole,

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:49.280
and it gives the atom
a potential energy so

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:52.480
that now the electromagnetic
force can act on it.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.160
The strength of the field
varies along that same pattern

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:58.000
as the standing wave.

00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:01.280
The electric field is weakest
at the nodes of the wave

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:03.680
and strongest at the antinodes.

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:06.560
This gives the dipole a potential
energy, causing the atom

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:07.920
to be drawn closer

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:09.760
and closer to the antinode

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:14.480
until the field strength
starts to level out.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.080
You can think of this like holding a

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.600
marble on the lip of a bowl.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:21.440
Here, the marble has maximum
gravitational potential energy,

00:12:21.440 --> 00:12:22.880
and when you let the marble go

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.920
and it moves down the slope,
the potential gets smaller

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.680
and smaller until at the bottom

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.840
of the bowl it reaches a minimum.

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:31.280
