00:23:43.120 --> 00:23:46.160
but we still need to be
within, I don't know,

00:23:46.160 --> 00:23:47.600
five megahertz of each other.

00:23:47.600 --> 00:23:50.800
- So pointing the beams at
each other isn't enough.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:52.000
The two lasers also have

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.120
to be at almost exactly
the same frequency.

00:23:55.120 --> 00:23:57.600
To put that in perspective,
a car radio only has to tune

00:23:57.600 --> 00:23:59.440
to within a fraction of
a percent of a station

00:23:59.440 --> 00:24:02.240
to pick it up, but these lasers have

00:24:02.240 --> 00:24:05.840
to match within a few
parts in a hundred million.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:07.040
So the terminal scans back

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:08.880
and forth, hunting for a window thousands

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:10.960
of times narrower than
the range it's searching.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:15.520
- Yeah, we've got 20 more
minutes of fuel left.

00:24:15.520 --> 00:24:19.840
- Does that include going
home time? I don't know.

00:24:19.840 --> 00:24:22.240
- And they proved the system
on the ground could find its

00:24:22.240 --> 00:24:25.680
partner in the sky, even if
they only got a few brief locks.

00:24:25.680 --> 00:24:28.160
It's not enough data yet to
properly compare the two clocks,

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:29.600
but it's a big step towards future

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:31.680
ground satellite transfers.

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:33.680
- In the end, it sounds easy,

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:37.120
but all we have to do is make
the mounts just track a bit

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:39.120
better and just a bit faster and stuff.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:41.440
And that's one thing that
we can easily do, you know,

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:43.320
with a bit more time and
a bit more engineering.

00:24:43.320 --> 00:24:45.680
- Since then, they've been up Mount Fuji,

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:47.040
testing their transfer system

00:24:47.040 --> 00:24:49.280
through two back-to-back typhoons.

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:54.320
- I think our target is just down here.

00:24:54.320 --> 00:24:58.000
Here's our terminal set up for the day.

00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:02.840
Beautiful views all
around. Absolutely amazing.

00:25:02.840 --> 00:25:06.320
- Their link will help Hidatoshi
Katori's group at Recon

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:08.000
and the University of
Tokyo do their own test

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:11.280
of general relativity in the spring.

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:14.640
- But there's one final
problem, and that's

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.760
because even if the link is
established successfully,

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:21.600
there are still 518
trillion oscillations coming

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:23.440
through those electronics every second.

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:25.280
And it turns out that there is

00:25:25.280 --> 00:25:27.120
no electronic device in the world

00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:29.760
that can read out signals this fast.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:32.800
Even some of the fastest
transistors can only switch on

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:36.080
and off about 10 billion times a second.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:39.680
And that's still 50,000 times too slow.

00:25:39.680 --> 00:25:42.400
So for decades, scientists
had a strange problem

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:45.600
because they could build
an optical atomic clock,

00:25:45.600 --> 00:25:48.720
they just had no way to
accurately measure its signal.

00:25:48.720 --> 00:25:52.320
What you'd need is some sort
of reference to tie it to

00:25:52.320 --> 00:25:55.280
at a frequency that you
already know exactly.

00:25:55.280 --> 00:25:56.480
For example, you could start

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:59.760
with a signal in the microwave
spectrum that was easy

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.440
and familiar to create

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:04.400
and then multiply its
frequency up in stages

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:06.720
until it reached the optical range,

00:26:06.720 --> 00:26:09.840
and then you could find the
exact frequency of the light

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.320
that you want to measure
from the optical atomic clock

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:15.920
by comparing it against the
signal that you generated.

00:26:15.920 --> 00:26:18.080
Okay, so I have two tuning forks here

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:20.720
and they're both at 440 hertz.

00:26:20.720 --> 00:26:22.000
And when I hit them, you can hear

00:26:22.000 --> 00:26:28.880
that they're both at the same frequency.

00:26:28.880 --> 00:26:31.280
Now I've put a small
weight on one of the forks

00:26:31.280 --> 00:26:33.200
and that is going to change
the frequency slightly,

00:26:33.200 --> 00:26:38.160
so listen to what happens now.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:42.480
You hear that? Sound.

00:26:44.320 --> 00:26:46.480
So what you're hearing is a new waveform

00:26:46.480 --> 00:26:48.720
that is pulsing at the
frequency difference

00:26:48.720 --> 00:26:50.560
between these two forks.

00:26:50.560 --> 00:26:52.080
It's called a beat node,

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:54.000
and the same thing can be done with light.

00:26:54.000 --> 00:26:56.320
You overlap two lasers
of different frequencies

00:26:56.320 --> 00:26:58.400
and you get a beat node too.

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:01.120
And the thing is, since
the frequency difference is

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:04.080
so much smaller than the signal
that we're trying to find,

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.560
we can actually read it
out using transistors

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:08.880
and we can do a simple calculation.

00:27:08.880 --> 00:27:10.400
The reference frequency

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:13.760
that you know plus the beat
note would equal the exact

00:27:13.760 --> 00:27:15.920
frequency of the unknown signal.

00:27:15.920 --> 00:27:19.600
But there's a problem. The
visible spectrum alone spans

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:21.840
around 400,000 gigahertz.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.280
It's extremely broad,

00:27:23.280 --> 00:27:26.560
and a beat node can only be
measured if the two signals are

00:27:26.560 --> 00:27:29.760
within a few tens of
gigahertz of each other.

00:27:29.760 --> 00:27:32.480
So the chances of your reference
signal being close enough

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:34.240
to the unknown signal that you're trying

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.400
to find are really small.

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:39.680
What scientists needed
wasn't one frequency,

00:27:39.680 --> 00:27:41.200
but many spread out so

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:43.760
that the unknown signal
would always sit close

00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:46.000
to at least one of them.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:48.480
So a reference signal is down
here in the microwave part

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:51.600
of the spectrum, so
let's call that F of R,

00:27:51.600 --> 00:27:55.200
and then our unknown signal
is somewhere up here in the

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:57.360
optical, so F of optical.

00:27:57.360 --> 00:28:01.040
So what I need is some
sort of ruler that is going

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:05.760
to be able to quantify this
difference in frequency

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:08.320
between my reference signal

00:28:08.320 --> 00:28:11.040
and my unknown signal of the
clock that I'm trying to find.

00:28:11.040 --> 00:28:13.920
In theory, you could set up
a number of lasers to act

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:17.200
as the notches of the ruler,
but this frequency in microwave

00:28:17.200 --> 00:28:19.840
and this frequency in
optical are very far apart.

00:28:19.840 --> 00:28:21.920
So if I want to bridge this gap,

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:24.080
I would need, well, a lot of lasers.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:27.040
So instead what would be useful
is if I could manipulate the

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:29.600
light of just a single
laser to produce all

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:32.960
of these different frequencies
at the same time, something

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:35.760
that would span all the
way from the microwaves

00:28:35.760 --> 00:28:37.680
that electronics could count up

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:40.320
to the light scientists wanted to measure.

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:44.480
In 1994, a German physicist
called Theodor Hänsch was,

00:28:44.480 --> 00:28:47.680
had a trade show when a
demonstration of a new type

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:50.080
of laser struck him with an idea.

00:28:50.080 --> 00:28:52.800
See, for decades, he'd been
coming up with different ways

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:55.680
to try and measure optical signals,

00:28:55.680 --> 00:28:59.600
but this particular titanium
sapphire laser could generate

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.440
incredibly short pulses of
light, each packed with a

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:05.600
vast range of frequencies.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.280
Now on a frequency graph,

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:10.880
that light looked like
the teeth of a comb.

00:29:10.880 --> 00:29:12.400
This could finally be the ruler

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:14.320
that everyone was searching for.

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:16.480
But to make the wide range

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:19.920
of frequencies an effective
ruler, each frequency needs

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:22.000
to be related to the other frequencies,

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:23.840
otherwise you can't really
predict the pattern.

00:29:23.840 --> 00:29:25.840
I mean, a ruler is only useful if these

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.160
notches are equally spaced.

00:29:28.160 --> 00:29:31.360
So Hench and his colleagues
put the laser to the test.

00:29:31.360 --> 00:29:32.480
They measured its light

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:34.720
and found that the frequencies contained

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:38.720
inside were perfectly evenly spaced.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:41.120
This is an optical frequency comb.

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:43.520
By 1999, a team in Boulder led

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:47.520
by John Hall had engineered
this comb into a ruler so wide

00:29:47.520 --> 00:29:52.160
that it could measure
almost any color of light.

00:29:52.160 --> 00:29:55.200
So now I have a comb with,
well, hundreds of thousands

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.520
of teeth, and some of them are
going to be close enough to

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:00.000
that unknown signal
that I'm trying to find.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.400
The comb is emitting its own frequency.

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:04.960
So there's going to be
a beat note that rings

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:08.640
between this comb tooth
and the unknown signal.

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:11.520
So by observing that beat
note with a photo diode,

00:30:11.520 --> 00:30:13.760
we have a formula to
calculate the frequency

00:30:13.760 --> 00:30:16.160
of our unknown optical signal.

00:30:16.160 --> 00:30:18.800
That's the tooth position
number multiplied

00:30:18.800 --> 00:30:22.560
by the repetition rate plus
the frequency of the beat note.

00:30:22.560 --> 00:30:24.480
And because the repetition rate is locked

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:27.920
to our reference signal, every
number in that formula is one

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:30.400
that electronics can measure.

00:30:30.400 --> 00:30:33.440
Within a year, frequency
combs were reading the first

00:30:33.440 --> 00:30:35.280
optical atomic clocks.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:39.440
Hänsch and Hall alongside Roy
Glauber were awarded the 2005

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:42.400
Nobel Prize for this work.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:45.440
On the mountain, each clock
signal is being sent over the

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:48.960
air using pulses of a frequency cone.

00:30:48.960 --> 00:30:50.320
General relativity predicts

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:52.560
that when you bring those pulses together,

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:54.640
the clock in the lab should take ever

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:58.800
so slightly slower compared
to the clock on the mountain.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:04.600
That is the theory. But what does the counter on the mountain actually say?

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:06.880
- So that is light over the link.

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.120
- So we locked in we found...
- We locked in. We found it.

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:13.120
So the fact that this is
moving slowly ramping
 - Yeah.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.200
That's the time output changing

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:17.840
because the clocks are
running at different rates.

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:21.840
So the time offset is slowly
ramping and we can see it more

00:31:21.840 --> 00:31:22.960
or less in real time.

00:31:22.960 --> 00:31:24.760
Here it goes.

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:28.960
- If Einstein is right, these
two clocks should differ

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:32.480
by 2.885 times 10 to the power

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:35.840
of negative 13 seconds every second.

00:31:35.840 --> 00:31:37.280
This number has been calculated

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:39.280
by another group based on measurements

00:31:39.280 --> 00:31:43.040
of the gravitational field in
the lab and on the mountain.

00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.640
And while both teams are working

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:46.880
to many more decimal places than this,

00:31:46.880 --> 00:31:49.840
they're actually keeping those
final digits secret from each

00:31:49.840 --> 00:31:52.560
other until they're ready
to compare findings.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:56.320
If the clocks measure a different
angle on the slope, well,

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:59.200
that's evidence that general
relativity might stop

00:31:59.200 --> 00:32:01.600
working at this level.

00:32:01.600 --> 00:32:04.160
So a few weeks later, we
hopped on a call with the team

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:07.520
to check out what the data is saying.

00:32:07.520 --> 00:32:09.059
Is Einstein still right?

00:32:09.059 --> 00:32:10.720
I'm going to answer that two ways.

00:32:10.720 --> 00:32:12.840
The first is a very simple
answer, which is yes.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.760
- Okay.
- We have no evidence that says no.

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:19.440
The second answer is that
we're still analyzing the data.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:23.120
So you're down at sort of the 13th digit

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:27.680
and a little change at
sort of the, a millionth of

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:30.080
that slope is what you're looking

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:31.600
for when you're looking for new physics.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.120
So it's a very small change.

00:32:33.120 --> 00:32:35.840
- I mean, it's incredible
that you have the sensitivity

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:38.160
to look at a millionth of, of this slope

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:40.720
that is already a trillionth of a second.

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:45.520
So wow. I mean that, that's
crazy. And are you guys excited?

00:32:45.520 --> 00:32:47.520
Are you nervous to, to know
that the data is there,

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.720
you just have to crunch the
numbers? Are you sleeping well?

00:32:49.720 --> 00:32:52.160
- You got to kind of, whatever it is,

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:53.680
put that number out there.

00:32:53.680 --> 00:32:56.400
And whether it agrees with
the classical prediction

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:59.800
or not, that's a little bit
of a nerve-wracking moment.

00:32:59.800 --> 00:33:01.200
- Yeah, I mean it's huge.

00:33:01.200 --> 00:33:03.360
- The best terrestrial test

00:33:03.360 --> 00:33:05.520
that's been done here
on Earth is about at the

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:06.960
100 part per million level.

00:33:06.960 --> 00:33:08.480
Our hope is that we'll be able

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:10.880
to do approaching the 10
part per million level

00:33:10.880 --> 00:33:12.960
with testing general relativity in a,

00:33:12.960 --> 00:33:14.400
in a new and deeper way.

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:17.280
- Have you got any more
of these tests planned in?

00:33:17.280 --> 00:33:18.800
- I think the big next step would be

00:33:18.800 --> 00:33:23.004
to try for a link to space.

00:33:27.486 --> 00:33:29.200
- It'll be a few months before Laura

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:31.680
and Andrew's team are ready
to share their final results.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:34.240
So we've linked their group's
page in the description if you

00:33:34.240 --> 00:33:36.160
want to follow their progress.

00:33:36.160 --> 00:33:38.400
I wanted to say a huge thank you to them

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:41.280
and the rest of the NIST
team and to Sasha Leilani

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:43.680
and the team in Perth for
all their help in this video.

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.320
And one final thing,
you might have noticed

00:33:46.320 --> 00:33:50.560
that we launched a second
channel called InGenius.

00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:00.400
Their recent episode was
about prediction markets

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:01.920
and who actually wins on them.

00:34:01.920 --> 00:34:04.960
Shockingly, it turns out it's
just the 0.1% at the top.

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:06.560
The team scoured three years worth

00:34:06.560 --> 00:34:08.480
of past trades on Polymarket to find out

00:34:08.480 --> 00:34:10.480
what really happens on the platform

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:12.400
and it's a really fascinating watch.

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:13.520
So if you're interested, I

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:14.720
really recommend you check it out.

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:16.240
I want to thank you for your support

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:23.120
and of course, as ever,
thank you for watching.

